  "explanation": {
    "short": "A syllable can end in an open vowel or a nasal sound (n, m, ng) and ring on: that is a \"live\" syllable (กิน, จาน). Or it can stop short with a short vowel or a closed p, t, k sound: that is a \"dead\" syllable (ปาก, ปิด, จะ).",
    "more": "With a mid-class letter, a live syllable is said in the mid tone (กิน), but a dead syllable drops to the low tone (ปาก, กบ, จะ). The letters ก ด บ at the end of a word are not said as g, d, b: they close the syllable with a k, t or p. So ปิด ends in t, จับ in p and ปาก in k. There are only these three stops at the end of a Thai syllable."
  },
